---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_451",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 451,
  "text": "[[2014.1]] <b>367₄.  De attributis, formis et de divisione corporum DE ATTRIBUTIS, FORMIS ET DE DIVISIONE CORPORUM </p>\n<p> [[2014.2]] <b>Attributa distincta</b> sunt quorum resolutio in natura priora intelligitur, seu quae\n[[2014.3]] definitione causali explicari possunt, ut Numerus, Magnitudo, Figura, Motus. <b>Confusa</b>\n[[2014.4]] sunt, quae explicari non possunt, sed aliis ut concipiantur, ostendi debent. Tales sunt\n[[2014.5]] qualitates sensuum extensorum, ut color, sonus, odor, sapor, vel explicantur a posteriori,\n[[2014.6]] ut calor, gravitas. </p>\n<p> [[2014.7]] <b>Attributum Homoeomeres</b>, quod parti et toti aeque tribuitur, ut extensio,\n[[2014.8]] motus, rubedo. In confusis attributis, qualis est rubedo, affirmari non potest, quod idem\n[[2014.9]] attributum cuilibet parti utcunque parvae competat, seu quod quaelibet pars rubri sit\n[[2014.10]] rubra; etsi hoc succedat in sensibilibus partibus. </p>\n<p> [[2015.1]] <b>Symbola</b> sunt corpora, quae in se mutuo permutari possunt, ut glacies et aqua. </p>\n<p> [[2015.2]] <b>Continua sunt corpora inter se</b>, quorum superficies aliqua communis est. Ubi\n[[2015.3]] notandum fieri non posse, ut duo corpora punctum tantum vel lineam tantum communem\n[[2015.4]] habeant. </p>\n<p> [[2015.5]] <b>Contigua</b> sunt corpora inter se quae terminos habent simul sive indistantes, nec\n[[2015.6]] refert isti termini sint puncta, an lineae, an superficies. </p>\n<p> [[2015.7]] <b>Corpus continuum</b> dicitur, cujus quaelibet partes contiguae per superficiem,\n[[2015.8]] sunt continuae inter se. </p>\n<p> [[2015.9]] <b>Corpora cohaerere</b> dicuntur, si uno moto sequatur alterum. Hinc si quid alteri\n[[2015.10]] inclusum sit, movetur quidem cum ipso, sed ipsum non sequitur. </p>\n<p> [[2015.11]] <b>Rarefit</b> corpus, si nullo novo corpore sensibili accedente, quod partibus ejus\n[[2015.12]] intercedat, ipsum corpus majorem quam ante locum occupat. </p>\n<p> [[2015.13]] <b>Condensatur</b> si nullo corpore sensibili inter partes ejus expulso ipsum corpus\n[[2015.14]] minorem quam antea locum occupet. Ita aër in thermometro modo majus modo minus\n[[2015.15]] spatium occupat. </p>\n<p> [[2015.16]] <b>Forma</b> est attributum abstractum absolutum, seu quod non concipitur ut modus\n[[2015.17]] entis, sed ut ens in se subsistens; exempli gratia nigredo. Nam ut ea vulgo concipitur ab\n[[2015.18]] hominibus, conceptus ejus nihil involvit quod eam per se extra molem corporis subsistere\n[[2015.19]] prohibeat. Sed si distincte concipiatur in quo consistat nigredo, fortasse deprehendetur\n[[2015.20]] eam necessario subjectum involvere, ut si nigredo sit cavernositas superficiei corporis,\n[[2015.21]] qua fiat ut radii lucis imbibantur, quo facto patet eam esse superficiei modum, ac proinde\n[[2015.22]] superficiem necessario involvere. Quoniam tamen saepe in scientiarum conclusionibus a\n[[2015.23]] causa hujus modi attributorum investiganda animum abstrahimus, ut in Mechanicis\n[[2015.24]] gravitatem saepe consideramus velut absolutam aliquam qualitatem corpori inhaerentem,\n[[2015.25]] perinde ac si corpus per se, sive vi hujus qualitatis intrinsecae, sine ulla externa causa ad\n[[2016.1]] centrum terrae tenderet; hinc attributa ista, quae saltem confuse instar absolutorum\n[[2016.2]] entium concipi possunt, a caeteris distinguere operae pretium fuit. (Omnis forma est\n[[2016.3]] attributum homoeomeres.) </p>\n<p> [[2016.4]] <b>Intensio</b> est quantitas formae in se, ut si forma sit motus, intensio erit celeritas. </p>\n<p> [[2016.5]] <b>Extensio formae</b> est quantitas materiae cui inest forma homoeomerica, ut\n[[2016.6]] quantitas corporis moti est ipsius motus extensio. </p>\n<p> [[2016.7]] <b>Quantitas formae</b> est factum ex intensione et extensione formae. </p>\n<p> [[2016.8]] Quantitas autem materiae fit ex spatio seu volumine ducto in densitatem corporis.\n[[2016.9]] Itaque Quantitas formae fit ex his tribus in se invicem ductis, volumine, materiae densitate,\n[[2016.10]] formae intensione. Exempli gratia pollex cubicus et pes cubicus aquae salsae tribus\n[[2016.11]] ex capitibus comparari possunt, nam et pes major pollice, et una aqua sale ditior altera\n[[2016.12]] esse potest (si nempe ex pollice cubico unius plus excoqui possit quam ex pollice cubico\n[[2016.13]] alterius) et denique sal ipse qui ex uno fit fortior esse potest quam qui ex altero elicietur. </p>\n<p> [[2016.14]] Corpora <b>per minima misceri</b> dicuntur si nulla pars compositi sumi potest, in\n[[2016.15]] quo non sit pars ex utroque componentium sumta. Ut si aqua sali misceatur. Non tamen\n[[2016.16]] hinc sequitur corpus hujusmodi esse similare, fieri enim potest ut diversis compositi\n[[2016.17]] partibus diversae sint partium ex componentibus sumtarum proportiones. Exempli causa\n[[2016.18]] si poneremus aquam profundiorem esse salsiorem, et semper salsedinem crescere cum\n[[2016.19]] profunditate. </p>\n<p> [[2016.20]] <b>Minima illa physica</b> sunt partes in quas unumquodque componentium in mixtione\n[[2016.21]] divisum est, sumuntur autem hic pro punctis, haec portio potest etiam dici Atomus. </p>\n<p> [[2016.22]] Si minimae partes in quas divisa sunt composita ponerentur esse puncta mathematica\n[[2016.23]] et poneretur etiam quodlibet compositi punctum esse mixtum, tunc perinde esset\n[[2016.24]] ac si quodlibet punctum componentium poneretur rarefactum, et pars una puncti rarefacti\n[[2016.25]] ex uno composita cum parte puncti rarefacti ex altero, rursus in punctum priori ante\n[[2016.26]] rarefactionem densitate aequale (vel si densitas utriusque inaequalis), mediae densitatis\n[[2017.1]] condensaretur. Hoc modo et formae et qualitates mixti essent factae ex formis et qualitatibus\n[[2017.2]] miscibilium inter se [rarefactis]. Sed haec cum aliunde constet secundum rigorem\n[[2017.3]] vera non esse, discutere nihil attinet. </p>\n",
  "number": "367₄.",
  "title": "De attributis, formis et de divisione corporum",
  "date": null
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:367₄. De attributis, formis et de divisione corporum DE ATTRIBUTIS, FORMIS ET DE DIVISIONE CORPORUM Attributa distincta sunt quorum resolutio in natura priora intelligitur, seu quae definitione causali explicari possunt, ut Numerus, Magnitudo, Figura, Motus. Confusa sunt, quae explicari non possunt,...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>